    "['isis enable [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent of the Huawei command `isis enable 1`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses logical unit numbers (like .0 for physical interfaces and .1 for loopback)\\n2. The ISIS protocol is enabled per interface in Juniper\\n3. There\\'s no direct \"enable\" keyword - interfaces are explicitly added under the IS-IS protocol\\n4. Loopback is typically \"lo0\" in Juniper rather than \"LoopBack1\"', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent of the Huawei command `isis enable 1`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses logical unit numbers (like .0 for physical interfaces and .1 for loopback)\\n2. The ISIS protocol is enabled per interface in Juniper\\n3. There\\'s no direct \"enable\" keyword - interfaces are explicitly added under the IS-IS protocol\\n4. Loopback is typically \"lo0\" in Juniper rather than \"LoopBack1\"', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.1', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent of the Huawei command `isis enable 1`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses logical unit numbers (like .0 for physical interfaces and .1 for loopback)\\n2. The ISIS protocol is enabled per interface in Juniper\\n3. There\\'s no direct \"enable\" keyword - interfaces are explicitly added under the IS-IS protocol\\n4. Loopback is typically \"lo0\" in Juniper rather than \"LoopBack1\"', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.1'}]]": 1,
    "['isis enable [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface <interface-name>', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'The equivalent Juniper configuration for the HUAWEI command `isis enable 1` would be enabling IS-IS on the interface with the appropriate instance. Here’s the translation:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface <interface-name>\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you would replace `<interface-name>` with the actual interface name (e.g., `ge-1/0/0`, `ge-2/0/0`, or `lo0.1` for the Loopback). Unlike HUAWEI, Juniper does not require an explicit IS-IS instance number (like `1`) on the interface level unless multiple instances are configured. \\n\\nFor a more precise mapping to the HUAWEI `isis enable 1` (which enables IS-IS instance 1 on the interface), if the Juniper IS-IS instance is named (e.g., `instance-1`), the command would be:\\n```\\nset protocols isis instance instance-1 interface <interface-name>\\n```\\n\\nHowever, since the original HUAWEI configuration does not specify an instance name, the first translation is the default equivalent.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface <interface-name>'}]]": 1,
    "['isis enable [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, enabling ISIS on an interface is done through the `protocols isis` hierarchy.\\n2. Each physical/logical interface needs to be explicitly listed under ISIS.\\n3. The `.0` suffix indicates the unit/interface number (common in Juniper configurations).\\n4. Loopback interfaces are typically named `lo0` in Juniper.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, enabling ISIS on an interface is done through the `protocols isis` hierarchy.\\n2. Each physical/logical interface needs to be explicitly listed under ISIS.\\n3. The `.0` suffix indicates the unit/interface number (common in Juniper configurations).\\n4. Loopback interfaces are typically named `lo0` in Juniper.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, enabling ISIS on an interface is done through the `protocols isis` hierarchy.\\n2. Each physical/logical interface needs to be explicitly listed under ISIS.\\n3. The `.0` suffix indicates the unit/interface number (common in Juniper configurations).\\n4. Loopback interfaces are typically named `lo0` in Juniper.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0'}]]": 1,
    "['isis prefix-sid absolute [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0 level 2 prefix-segment index 153710', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent command for the Huawei `isis prefix-sid absolute 153710` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0 level 2 prefix-segment index 153710\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, the Loopback interface is typically `lo0.0`\\n- The `prefix-segment index` command is Juniper's equivalent to Huawei's `prefix-sid`\\n- The value `153710` remains the same absolute SID value\\n- The `level 2` specification matches the IS-IS level from the original configuration\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0 level 2 prefix-segment index 153710'}]]": 1
